#{[17592186385878 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385878 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385878 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7cba45f1-f7ac-5129-91d4-822862ce8124" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385879 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385879 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "758ea834-5946-5f25-9e85-8f4913820e94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385879 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-16I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-16I=[\\begin{aligned}20 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 32\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385880 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385880 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385880 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186385883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385880 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a3fc05ae-4f60-5971-aae9-bcab380baf50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385881 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385881 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385881 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7298123d-e222-5ca3-9525-3902520c925c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385882 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385882 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385882 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a7e67d4a-7cab-5968-8a1e-93e5b46651cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385883 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385883 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385883 #edb/ref 1083 #