t{17}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9020d5f8-c5f3-54dc-9474-2d2b14ec7c2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186428016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186428017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186428018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218642800